00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:22.322
Thank you for that one "oh."

00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:22.856
It's OK.

00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:26.059
I'll take what I can get.

00:00:26.059 --> 00:00:32.432
It's our last Friday, and so
next week is our last week.

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:34.367
We have class on
Monday, and Wednesday

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:37.337
will be the last class.

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:40.040
And we've got one
more smallish topic

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.309
to cover after this,
which is diffusion,

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:46.212
and we'll wrap up on Wednesday.

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:49.082
But today, I want to keep
talking about polymers.

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:51.418
So this is our polymer week.

00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:53.653
And I want to go back
to the properties

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:57.257
I've put here, the properties
we've talked about.

00:00:57.257 --> 00:00:57.757
Right?

00:00:57.757 --> 00:01:02.595
So on Monday, we focused
on what a polymer is,

00:01:02.595 --> 00:01:05.698
and we focused on
the ways you make it.

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:06.232
All right?

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:11.170
The radical initiation, chain,
addition, and the condensation

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:14.207
polymers that you know,
the condensation where

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:16.709
you have two different mers.

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:20.947
And then on Wednesday, we talked
about things that matter, kind

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:22.582
of like engineering polymers.

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:27.454
Like what are the properties
of the polymers that you

00:01:27.454 --> 00:01:30.056
can change, that we can change?

00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:30.657
All right?

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:32.092
And so we talked
about, well, you

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:33.660
could pick a different monomer.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:34.994
That will change the properties.

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:35.527
Right?

00:01:35.527 --> 00:01:37.130
We've been through that.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.934
You could try to grow
longer or shorter strands,

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:43.402
so that's going to
change properties.

00:01:43.402 --> 00:01:47.006
The interactions
between the strands,

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:50.577
which will, of course, depend
on what you pick there.

00:01:50.577 --> 00:01:52.512
Right?

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:56.583
And then we talked about the
density and the crystallinity

00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:58.318
and how that could
depend on things

00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:02.655
like cooling rate, or maybe
the physical structure itself

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:03.823
of the chain.

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:04.891
So what do I mean by that?

00:02:04.891 --> 00:02:07.060
Well, when I say
physical structure,

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:08.728
of course I mean
the chemistry that

00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:12.765
makes that physical structure,
so I could branch the chain

00:02:12.765 --> 00:02:15.335
with the same monomers,
but now instead

00:02:15.335 --> 00:02:18.571
of being one linear chain
it's a bunch of branches.

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:21.808
Or maybe you could
have a functional group

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:24.010
that you can control which
side of the chain it's on.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.412
And all those things
would lead to differences

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:30.083
in density an crystallinity.

00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:32.852
And then the last thing we
talked about is cross-linking.

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:34.420
And what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:37.991
to add one more thing
to this list today,

00:02:37.991 --> 00:02:39.759
but before I do that
I want to pick up

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:43.228
on the last slide of
Wednesday, which is this.

00:02:43.228 --> 00:02:46.666
So we tend-- so all this
kind of goes in together

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:49.802
to give you these
solids called polymers.

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:50.870
Right?

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.174
But we talked about
how sometimes they're

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:56.509
not fully solid,
and that's what's

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:57.677
going to come in the middle.

00:02:57.677 --> 00:03:01.314
But you can make a
solid thermoplastic,

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:02.682
which is these strands.

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:03.183
Right?

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:04.250
So what is a thermal?

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.419
Ah, what's the?

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:09.756
Anyway, so with thermoplastic
we'll look over here.

00:03:09.756 --> 00:03:13.960
There is no cross-linking, so
you don't have any of this.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:14.961
But what do I have?

00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:17.564
Well, I have these
really long strands

00:03:17.564 --> 00:03:20.900
that are bonded together
with these IMFs,

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.671
typically in a
thermoplastic you would

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:27.473
have something like
maybe van der Waals,

00:03:27.473 --> 00:03:30.577
and maybe you have some H bonds.

00:03:30.577 --> 00:03:35.915
And these materials, because
you didn't cross-link them,

00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:38.117
and you didn't
cross-link them strongly

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:40.420
with some kind of strong
bond, because of that,

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.322
you can reheat them
and reprocess them.

00:03:42.322 --> 00:03:45.692
So these are actually
often the plastics

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:49.128
that we recycle
that we can recycle.

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:51.831
And like I said, they're mostly
linear and maybe slightly

00:03:51.831 --> 00:03:54.801
branched polymers,
and they're used

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:59.005
in a whole lot of applications,
so we call them thermoplastics.

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:01.474
Thermal-- you can heat them.

00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:01.975
Right?

00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:03.977
You can heat them,
and you can see

00:04:03.977 --> 00:04:07.480
what happens is if I
heat these back up,

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.115
these links can start moving.

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:12.619
They wiggle, and they wiggle
until they can just kind

00:04:12.619 --> 00:04:18.324
of slide past each other
and become a vicious liquid

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:19.625
and then a melt.

00:04:19.625 --> 00:04:24.230
On the other hand, if
I've got a thermoset,

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:28.801
I heated it up, and then as
it set I crossed linked it.

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:30.737
And there are lots of
ways you can cross-link.

00:04:30.737 --> 00:04:32.205
We talked about some of those.

00:04:32.205 --> 00:04:34.207
Right?

00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:36.709
And now I've got a pretty
high density of cross-links,

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:38.411
so that's drawn here.

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:40.179
So I've got these long strands.

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:42.548
Remember, they're super,
super long, right?

00:04:42.548 --> 00:04:47.186
But now, every so often I
have something linking them

00:04:47.186 --> 00:04:48.321
together.

00:04:48.321 --> 00:04:50.089
And if I put a lot
of cross-links in,

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:51.524
and if they're
strong cross-links,

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:54.460
then you can get
really hard plastics.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.094
Right?

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:58.064
And so these are high--
the thermoset, they're set.

00:04:58.064 --> 00:04:59.365
They're set.

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:01.601
Thermoset, good.

00:05:01.601 --> 00:05:04.570
It solidifies, and
it can't be reheated.

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:05.138
Why?

00:05:05.138 --> 00:05:05.772
Think about it.

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:11.344
If I heat that up, then, OK,
I might melt the polymers,

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:13.212
so the chains are kind
of wiggling around,

00:05:13.212 --> 00:05:16.816
but those cross-links
aren't going anywhere.

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:18.818
They're really strong.

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:20.286
And so what happens
with thermosets

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:22.689
is they're very
difficult to recycle.

00:05:22.689 --> 00:05:25.291
They're very difficult to
recycle because what'll happen

00:05:25.291 --> 00:05:29.629
is before those things melt
the whole thing catches fire.

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:30.863
The whole thing catches fire.

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:32.765
It burns often.

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:35.368
Or maybe it doesn't burn,
but when you melt it,

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:37.337
you've got this
weird chemistry now

00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:40.173
of the cross-linker
mixed into the polymer,

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:42.008
and it's not useful anymore.

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:44.010
So these kinds of plastics--

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.511
the cross-link is really great.

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:49.615
You make, you know, like I
said, over 30% of all toys.

00:05:49.615 --> 00:05:51.551
It's used in many,
many applications,

00:05:51.551 --> 00:05:55.855
these harder plastics, but
they're not recyclable.

00:05:55.855 --> 00:05:59.392
And then, we talked about
this intermediate region.

00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:00.526
And this is where we ended.

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:05.798
We talked about elastomers,
and elastomers are in between.

00:06:05.798 --> 00:06:11.170
And so in an elastomer, you've
got light cross-linking.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:13.940
And the cross-linking,
like in slime,

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:18.911
that cross-linking has
a weaker hydrogen bond.

00:06:18.911 --> 00:06:19.579
All right?

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:20.980
And remember, we
talked about how

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:23.816
that could lead to
viscoelasticity, really

00:06:23.816 --> 00:06:26.686
cool stuff.

00:06:26.686 --> 00:06:32.325
In other cases, like in the
case of the vulcanized rubber--

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:33.659
remember that, Charles Goodyear?

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:35.128
That story from Wednesday?

00:06:35.128 --> 00:06:37.130
In that case, you
put a covalent bond

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:42.235
of sulfur between the
strands, and that gave you

00:06:42.235 --> 00:06:47.874
a much stronger rubber that
still had some elasticity.

00:06:47.874 --> 00:06:51.377
But now, the best analogy
here is still the spaghetti.

00:06:51.377 --> 00:06:53.146
I keep coming back to it.

00:06:53.146 --> 00:06:54.814
I'm not letting go.

00:06:54.814 --> 00:06:56.115
Think about it.

00:06:56.115 --> 00:07:01.687
If I've got a bowl of
spaghetti, and I pull on it,

00:07:01.687 --> 00:07:04.190
that's my plastic
bag on the left.

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:04.690
All right?

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:05.591
That's my plastic bag.

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:07.360
I pull on it, and
those chains are all

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:10.563
crumpled up and tangled,
and I'm untangling them,

00:07:10.563 --> 00:07:12.832
and then I'm sliding
them past each other,

00:07:12.832 --> 00:07:15.101
and pulling them until
the whole thing rips.

00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:16.202
That's the bowl spaghetti.

00:07:16.202 --> 00:07:20.807
But now I've got this spaghetti
that attaches every so often,

00:07:20.807 --> 00:07:22.942
one strand to another.

00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:23.576
Think about it.

00:07:23.576 --> 00:07:27.113
I've got a bowl spaghetti, but
in each strand of spaghetti,

00:07:27.113 --> 00:07:29.148
there's like five places
where it's attached

00:07:29.148 --> 00:07:30.416
to another strand of spaghetti.

00:07:30.416 --> 00:07:31.751
Now what happens?

00:07:31.751 --> 00:07:34.954
I can pull that as well.

00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:36.522
I can pull it because
those spaghetti

00:07:36.522 --> 00:07:39.459
these are all curled
up, and so there's

00:07:39.459 --> 00:07:42.929
some amount of pulling
that I can do while they

00:07:42.929 --> 00:07:47.133
uncurl until those
links take over,

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:49.302
and I can't pull past them.

00:07:49.302 --> 00:07:50.002
All right?

00:07:50.002 --> 00:07:54.273
That's what the light
cross-linking will do.

00:07:54.273 --> 00:07:56.808
And you say, well, if I
had heavy cross-linking

00:07:56.808 --> 00:07:58.978
in the spaghetti, then the
whole bowl of spaghetti

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:01.013
wouldn't be very easy
to move because I'm

00:08:01.013 --> 00:08:04.083
always up against all
the links between them.

00:08:04.083 --> 00:08:05.251
All right?

00:08:05.251 --> 00:08:08.054
And so that's why these
elastomers are so interesting,

00:08:08.054 --> 00:08:10.456
because they fall--
it's all interesting.

00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:11.924
But the elastomers
fall in between,

00:08:11.924 --> 00:08:15.194
where there's some of that
uncurling of the polymers

00:08:15.194 --> 00:08:17.930
until you get to a point where,
depending on how much you've

00:08:17.930 --> 00:08:20.299
cross-linked, and the
bonding of the cross-link,

00:08:20.299 --> 00:08:22.902
you're going to go up
against the cross-linking.

00:08:22.902 --> 00:08:23.970
All right?

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:26.572
And so these are used,
so they're free to move.

00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:29.275
Now, must be above its glass
transition temperature.

00:08:29.275 --> 00:08:31.210
I want to talk about
that for a second

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:32.578
because I did
mention this, and I

00:08:32.578 --> 00:08:34.780
want to be sure that we all
have a good sense of what

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:35.881
that means.

00:08:35.881 --> 00:08:40.520
And so I wanted to--
taking this elastomer,

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.755
say, what happens
when you melt it?

00:08:42.755 --> 00:08:43.256
All right?

00:08:43.256 --> 00:08:49.195
And we drew this curve, but
I want to go very carefully.

00:08:49.195 --> 00:08:50.196
This is the temperature.

00:08:50.196 --> 00:08:55.067
Oh, we love this curve,
the molar volume.

00:08:55.067 --> 00:08:55.635
Right?

00:08:55.635 --> 00:09:00.806
And now up here,
you've got your liquid.

00:09:00.806 --> 00:09:01.707
Right?

00:09:01.707 --> 00:09:02.942
We know that.

00:09:02.942 --> 00:09:06.612
And then here is the place
where it would crystallize.

00:09:10.249 --> 00:09:11.417
Can a polymer crystallize?

00:09:11.417 --> 00:09:12.318
Sure.

00:09:12.318 --> 00:09:12.818
Right.

00:09:12.818 --> 00:09:14.387
If a polymer
crystallized, then it

00:09:14.387 --> 00:09:16.889
would just be these
strands in a crystal.

00:09:16.889 --> 00:09:17.957
What is a crystal?

00:09:17.957 --> 00:09:21.460
We know it's a repeating
ordered structure.

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:23.663
Yes, polymers can crystallize.

00:09:23.663 --> 00:09:26.899
But as we've talked
about so often, what

00:09:26.899 --> 00:09:33.406
happens is because, like we
talked about with silica,

00:09:33.406 --> 00:09:35.908
you know, you've got this
very viscous, amorphous,

00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:37.577
can't find the crystal sites--

00:09:37.577 --> 00:09:38.811
same with polymers.

00:09:38.811 --> 00:09:41.814
And so you get a glass.

00:09:41.814 --> 00:09:44.383
And then this was the Tg.

00:09:44.383 --> 00:09:45.518
Now, why am I showing this?

00:09:45.518 --> 00:09:48.387
Because with these strands--

00:09:48.387 --> 00:09:49.522
and I want to show you.

00:09:49.522 --> 00:09:53.225
You know, we've drawn this
before where often what you

00:09:53.225 --> 00:09:55.461
get in polymers is some degree.

00:09:55.461 --> 00:09:58.965
It's right here, some
degree of crystallinity.

00:09:58.965 --> 00:10:00.132
Right?

00:10:00.132 --> 00:10:01.801
So what do I do?

00:10:01.801 --> 00:10:03.769
How do I do this?

00:10:03.769 --> 00:10:07.039
Well, you literally have both.

00:10:07.039 --> 00:10:07.873
All right.

00:10:07.873 --> 00:10:09.075
So I've drawn this already.

00:10:09.075 --> 00:10:13.245
So if I'm here on
my polymer, then it

00:10:13.245 --> 00:10:15.581
might look like what I've
drawn already on the board.

00:10:15.581 --> 00:10:16.749
Oh, I love doing this.

00:10:16.749 --> 00:10:19.452
And then there's the crystal,
and then there's more.

00:10:22.121 --> 00:10:23.122
I've drawn this already.

00:10:23.122 --> 00:10:23.155
Right?

00:10:23.155 --> 00:10:25.024
You've got this
crystalline region,

00:10:25.024 --> 00:10:29.028
so you've got the
crystal, and here,

00:10:29.028 --> 00:10:31.831
because I'm below the glass
transition temperature,

00:10:31.831 --> 00:10:34.767
this part here is an amorphous--

00:10:34.767 --> 00:10:39.338
oh boy, it's kind of small, but
this word says "amorphous"--

00:10:39.338 --> 00:10:39.839
solid.

00:10:42.375 --> 00:10:44.043
It's a solid.

00:10:44.043 --> 00:10:46.812
That part is a solid because
it's below the glass transition

00:10:46.812 --> 00:10:47.613
temperature.

00:10:47.613 --> 00:10:48.514
All right?

00:10:48.514 --> 00:10:51.250
OK, but now let's
start heating this up.

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:52.785
And as I get to the--

00:10:52.785 --> 00:10:56.022
so now I'm going to get here.

00:10:56.022 --> 00:10:58.790
And what happens is
it doesn't actually

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:04.096
go straight to the full liquid.

00:11:04.096 --> 00:11:07.667
I mean, it's got this
crystalline stuff in there,

00:11:07.667 --> 00:11:08.234
right?

00:11:08.234 --> 00:11:09.869
It's got these regions.

00:11:09.869 --> 00:11:14.874
This crystal has
one melting point.

00:11:14.874 --> 00:11:16.976
Remember, the melting
point of the crystal

00:11:16.976 --> 00:11:19.412
is the melting point
of the crystal.

00:11:19.412 --> 00:11:21.380
It doesn't change.

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:24.684
Glass transition
temperature can be tuned.

00:11:24.684 --> 00:11:25.651
But this is a melting--

00:11:25.651 --> 00:11:27.153
Now, so what happens.

00:11:27.153 --> 00:11:30.756
Well, what happens is there's
more of a curvyness to this,

00:11:30.756 --> 00:11:34.160
and you might kind of have
the shape look more like this.

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:39.598
So what's happening?

00:11:39.598 --> 00:11:42.101
This just gets a little
bit more at the detail

00:11:42.101 --> 00:11:43.769
of how a polymer
would melt if it's

00:11:43.769 --> 00:11:45.137
got any crystalline regions.

00:11:45.137 --> 00:11:47.440
And as I've told you,
very often it does.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:50.376
Not always-- it could have
no crystalline regions.

00:11:50.376 --> 00:11:53.479
It could be totally amorphous,
but I've drawn this enough

00:11:53.479 --> 00:11:56.182
that I wanted to explain
this curve with that

00:11:56.182 --> 00:11:58.017
as our starting point.

00:11:58.017 --> 00:11:59.452
All right?

00:11:59.452 --> 00:12:01.854
And I've also talked about how
the degree of crystallinity

00:12:01.854 --> 00:12:04.890
is so important for properties,
and it can be controlled.

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:05.958
So what's happening?

00:12:05.958 --> 00:12:09.195
Well, in this region here--
let's draw that picture.

00:12:09.195 --> 00:12:10.496
So now I'm in here.

00:12:10.496 --> 00:12:15.067
And what happens is I'm
below Tm, but I'm above Tg.

00:12:15.067 --> 00:12:19.238
So that means that, literally
in the same strand--

00:12:19.238 --> 00:12:21.774
this is so cool.

00:12:21.774 --> 00:12:24.243
That looks the same.

00:12:24.243 --> 00:12:29.882
It's not supposed to be the same
because now this is a liquid.

00:12:29.882 --> 00:12:39.391
This is a viscous liquid,
and this is still a crystal.

00:12:39.391 --> 00:12:42.228
That's an xtol.

00:12:42.228 --> 00:12:44.563
I'm below Tm.

00:12:44.563 --> 00:12:46.732
So the crystalline
region has not melted,

00:12:46.732 --> 00:12:50.102
but the rest of the polymer
is above its glass transition.

00:12:50.102 --> 00:12:51.103
This is so cool.

00:12:51.103 --> 00:12:55.908
This one strand is both a
solid and a liquid, one strand.

00:12:55.908 --> 00:12:58.277
That's so cool.

00:12:58.277 --> 00:12:59.812
That's in here.

00:12:59.812 --> 00:13:00.312
All right?

00:13:00.312 --> 00:13:04.083
And then I get over
here, and now here--

00:13:04.083 --> 00:13:05.818
OK, well, let's see.

00:13:05.818 --> 00:13:08.854
So now this is all liquid,
and the crystalline part

00:13:08.854 --> 00:13:10.589
is starting to come apart.

00:13:10.589 --> 00:13:16.929
So crystal melts, xtol melts.

00:13:16.929 --> 00:13:18.664
All right, well,
that sort of was

00:13:18.664 --> 00:13:21.867
supposed to be the
remnants of a crystal.

00:13:21.867 --> 00:13:25.404
And then above Tm, the whole
thing's a liquid, right?

00:13:25.404 --> 00:13:26.739
So that's kind of zooming in.

00:13:26.739 --> 00:13:28.407
I wanted to make sure
that we understood

00:13:28.407 --> 00:13:31.110
this plot in the context
of these polymers.

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:33.145
And that also
gives you a sense--

00:13:33.145 --> 00:13:36.682
OK, now, that's this one strand.

00:13:36.682 --> 00:13:38.083
Now, I've got a
bunch of strands.

00:13:38.083 --> 00:13:40.419
Did I connect them or not?

00:13:40.419 --> 00:13:43.455
And that adds the layer
of what an elastomer is.

00:13:43.455 --> 00:13:43.956
Right?

00:13:43.956 --> 00:13:47.660
Because now you can
imagine, is the elastomer

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:49.795
above or below Tg?

00:13:49.795 --> 00:13:50.596
Where's is Tg?

00:13:50.596 --> 00:13:55.134
Are we above or below Tg
when we bounce a ball?

00:13:55.134 --> 00:13:56.402
Right?

00:13:56.402 --> 00:14:01.040
How does that influence
the properties of the ball?

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:05.077
A rubber ball is
likely cross-linked.

00:14:05.077 --> 00:14:08.848
And you can imagine
that if you're below Tg,

00:14:08.848 --> 00:14:14.620
then all that amorphous
part of the ball is a solid,

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:16.488
so there's an elastic response.

00:14:16.488 --> 00:14:19.291
Sure, it could still bounce.

00:14:19.291 --> 00:14:23.829
If you're in this region,
then that part of the ball

00:14:23.829 --> 00:14:25.731
is a liquid, but it's
likely cross-linked

00:14:25.731 --> 00:14:28.100
so the ball doesn't fall apart.

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:29.301
Right?

00:14:29.301 --> 00:14:31.003
So it's going to
bounce differently.

00:14:31.003 --> 00:14:32.371
Right?

00:14:32.371 --> 00:14:34.740
It's going to
bounce differently.

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:37.009
OK, so anyway, I
wanted to just go

00:14:37.009 --> 00:14:40.613
into detail of that to make
sure that we feel our oneness.

00:14:40.613 --> 00:14:43.115
And I mean, you could add here.

00:14:43.115 --> 00:14:47.219
This is, because it's
so important in just--

00:14:47.219 --> 00:14:50.522
like I was saying, just in
terms of what this material, how

00:14:50.522 --> 00:14:52.925
this material behaves, you
could add the glass transition

00:14:52.925 --> 00:14:57.563
temperature, which, as
we've already talked about,

00:14:57.563 --> 00:15:00.966
has a number of ways
that it can be tuned.

00:15:00.966 --> 00:15:02.768
On the other hand,
it may also be

00:15:02.768 --> 00:15:05.070
tuned by some of these
other things that you do,

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:08.440
but it's a very important
part of the polymer property

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:10.142
ecosystem.

00:15:10.142 --> 00:15:11.243
OK.

00:15:11.243 --> 00:15:11.911
Good.

00:15:11.911 --> 00:15:14.146
So that's the elastomer.

00:15:14.146 --> 00:15:15.781
Now, there's one more
thing that we can

00:15:15.781 --> 00:15:17.683
do that I wanted to talk about.

00:15:17.683 --> 00:15:18.918
Oh, that's the picture.

00:15:18.918 --> 00:15:20.853
Yeah, I thought I'd show
you another picture.

00:15:20.853 --> 00:15:24.123
So I'm not the only one
that draws things like this.

00:15:24.123 --> 00:15:24.757
All right?

00:15:24.757 --> 00:15:27.660
OK, there's a nice one from
Encyclopedia Britannica,

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:32.331
and you can imagine this
region being a solid down here,

00:15:32.331 --> 00:15:33.132
then a liquid.

00:15:33.132 --> 00:15:35.467
But that's still solid,
and then that region

00:15:35.467 --> 00:15:38.103
melts all at once at Tm.

00:15:38.103 --> 00:15:39.571
The last thing
that I want to add

00:15:39.571 --> 00:15:44.843
is another way that we have
to control polymer properties,

00:15:44.843 --> 00:15:50.049
and it's another bullet here,
and it'll be our last one.

00:15:50.049 --> 00:15:53.385
And it's really the
composition and sequence.

00:15:56.889 --> 00:15:58.924
So what do I mean by that?

00:15:58.924 --> 00:16:04.763
Well, it turns out that--

00:16:04.763 --> 00:16:07.599
remember when we
did condensation.

00:16:07.599 --> 00:16:08.100
Right?

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:11.103
Polymerization, a poly
condensation it's called.

00:16:11.103 --> 00:16:12.604
The condensation polymerization.

00:16:12.604 --> 00:16:15.374
We had two different mers.

00:16:15.374 --> 00:16:17.009
And we could have
picked the box.

00:16:17.009 --> 00:16:20.713
Remember, the box inside the
mer could be different things.

00:16:20.713 --> 00:16:23.515
And then when we've done
the radical initiation,

00:16:23.515 --> 00:16:25.718
we've just had one mer
with the double bond.

00:16:25.718 --> 00:16:26.618
Right?

00:16:26.618 --> 00:16:28.287
Now, if you have
one mer, then that's

00:16:28.287 --> 00:16:33.425
called a homopolymer, Homopoly.

00:16:33.425 --> 00:16:35.861
And if you have two,
it's called a copolymer.

00:16:39.631 --> 00:16:43.669
The thing is that we actually
can even make copolymers

00:16:43.669 --> 00:16:46.171
with both approaches.

00:16:46.171 --> 00:16:48.607
And not only that, but
we're learning more and more

00:16:48.607 --> 00:16:53.946
how to control the sequence,
and that's what this shows here.

00:16:53.946 --> 00:16:57.349
So I could have a polymer A.

00:16:57.349 --> 00:17:00.986
Let's say I have a--

00:17:00.986 --> 00:17:02.955
yeah, I'll put it below this.

00:17:02.955 --> 00:17:08.493
Let's say I have a, you know,
polymer, polyethylene, PE,

00:17:08.493 --> 00:17:10.863
which is-- oh, we know
now how to draw these--

00:17:10.863 --> 00:17:17.770
C, C, N. And then I've
got the other one, which

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:21.607
is PVC polyvinyl chloride,
and that looks like this--

00:17:21.607 --> 00:17:26.111
C-- all I've done here is swap
out a hydrogen for a chlorine,

00:17:26.111 --> 00:17:29.448
and I've got a totally
different polymer.

00:17:29.448 --> 00:17:30.349
All right?

00:17:30.349 --> 00:17:32.651
Some other end, OK?

00:17:32.651 --> 00:17:35.187
And an N, whatever.

00:17:35.187 --> 00:17:37.356
I can now alternate these.

00:17:37.356 --> 00:17:39.558
I can alternate them, and
I could alternate them

00:17:39.558 --> 00:17:40.059
in this way.

00:17:40.059 --> 00:17:44.196
So PE might be my A,
and PVC might be my B.

00:17:44.196 --> 00:17:46.532
And if I alternate them,
we might-- you know,

00:17:46.532 --> 00:17:48.000
if it's like
regular alternating,

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:49.468
we might write PE--

00:17:49.468 --> 00:17:53.705
this is how we'd write
the copolymer-- a, PVC.

00:17:53.705 --> 00:17:56.675
All right, but you could
also have it be random,

00:17:56.675 --> 00:17:58.043
so then you'd write PE--

00:17:58.043 --> 00:18:01.814
you can take a guess-- r, PVC.

00:18:01.814 --> 00:18:03.215
Right?

00:18:03.215 --> 00:18:06.318
You could write a
grafted version--

00:18:06.318 --> 00:18:09.488
PE, g-- well, you
get the point--

00:18:09.488 --> 00:18:11.490
PVC, and so forth.

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:14.359
What I mean when I
write this is that I've

00:18:14.359 --> 00:18:17.963
taken these two polymers,
and I've copolymerized.

00:18:17.963 --> 00:18:19.231
I mean, sorry, these two.

00:18:19.231 --> 00:18:20.899
Well yeah, these
are the polymers.

00:18:20.899 --> 00:18:23.268
I'm taking the
monomers, and I've

00:18:23.268 --> 00:18:26.004
made one polymer out
of the two of them,

00:18:26.004 --> 00:18:27.873
but I've controlled
the sequence.

00:18:27.873 --> 00:18:28.407
It's not--

00:18:28.407 --> 00:18:29.942
Now, maybe it's not controlled.

00:18:29.942 --> 00:18:33.245
Maybe it's random right there.

00:18:33.245 --> 00:18:36.448
Or maybe I've figured out
how to do this in a way

00:18:36.448 --> 00:18:39.618
that I have the
backbone all one type,

00:18:39.618 --> 00:18:41.153
but I can have
these side chains,

00:18:41.153 --> 00:18:43.889
the branches, another type.

00:18:43.889 --> 00:18:46.692
That's really
powerful, it turns out.

00:18:46.692 --> 00:18:50.429
Or maybe you can control that I
have a certain number of them,

00:18:50.429 --> 00:18:52.064
and then a certain
number of the other,

00:18:52.064 --> 00:18:55.834
and this is called
a block copolymer.

00:18:55.834 --> 00:18:57.469
And some of you may
have heard of block

00:18:57.469 --> 00:19:01.707
copolymer chemistry,
which is growing,

00:19:01.707 --> 00:19:04.810
is a very growing,
very powerful field.

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:06.945
Because when you
make these blocks

00:19:06.945 --> 00:19:09.381
and you control
their properties,

00:19:09.381 --> 00:19:11.550
you can imagine that you
control all sorts of things

00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:14.586
about how that polymer behaves.

00:19:14.586 --> 00:19:19.057
Imagine you make something
that bonds to A but not B.

00:19:19.057 --> 00:19:21.193
Like dissolves like.

00:19:21.193 --> 00:19:22.761
All right, we've been there.

00:19:22.761 --> 00:19:26.765
B is something that
bonds to B but not A. OK,

00:19:26.765 --> 00:19:27.733
what's going to happen?

00:19:27.733 --> 00:19:30.736
They're stuck, but
they're going to try

00:19:30.736 --> 00:19:34.606
to come together and stay
apart in the same strand.

00:19:34.606 --> 00:19:36.675
All sorts of interesting
things can happen.

00:19:36.675 --> 00:19:40.412
It can be engineered
when you can control

00:19:40.412 --> 00:19:43.749
these blocks or these graphs.

00:19:43.749 --> 00:19:45.350
All right?

00:19:45.350 --> 00:19:47.853
So that's the last
thing that we--

00:19:47.853 --> 00:19:49.121
Here's an example.

00:19:49.121 --> 00:19:51.356
This is-- I was trying
to look for a good exam--

00:19:51.356 --> 00:19:53.358
here's something called--

00:19:53.358 --> 00:19:54.526
actually, what is it called?

00:19:54.526 --> 00:19:55.661
Surlyn.

00:19:55.661 --> 00:19:56.795
Who comes up with that?

00:19:56.795 --> 00:19:59.064
I don't know why that's the
name, but that's the name.

00:19:59.064 --> 00:20:02.901
It's the Surlyn resin,
which is the ingredient that

00:20:02.901 --> 00:20:06.672
goes into the polymer, OK?

00:20:06.672 --> 00:20:08.674
And look at what it does.

00:20:08.674 --> 00:20:13.045
Oh, it provides clarity,
toughness, versatility.

00:20:13.045 --> 00:20:17.216
Surlyn is a leading choice for
food, cosmetic, medical device,

00:20:17.216 --> 00:20:23.288
skin, stretch, packaging,
as well as golf balls.

00:20:23.288 --> 00:20:24.556
The tunability is enor--

00:20:24.556 --> 00:20:25.057
why?

00:20:25.057 --> 00:20:27.092
What is Surlyn?

00:20:27.092 --> 00:20:30.128
Well, we don't know exactly
because they won't tell us,

00:20:30.128 --> 00:20:32.798
but Surlyn is
actually really cool.

00:20:32.798 --> 00:20:36.235
It's using-- OK, it's using--

00:20:36.235 --> 00:20:37.035
where?

00:20:37.035 --> 00:20:38.270
Dah!

00:20:38.270 --> 00:20:39.071
Where did I put it?

00:20:39.071 --> 00:20:42.374
Composition, sequence--
it's using sequence

00:20:42.374 --> 00:20:48.046
to make a graft polymer
where one type is

00:20:48.046 --> 00:20:50.349
going along the chain and
the other type is coming out.

00:20:50.349 --> 00:20:54.052
But really importantly,
the other type

00:20:54.052 --> 00:20:58.257
is made specifically
to form ionic bonds.

00:20:58.257 --> 00:21:01.159
So here B is made
so that it can form

00:21:01.159 --> 00:21:04.329
ionic bonds with other parts.

00:21:04.329 --> 00:21:09.668
Ionic, remember, that's
basically by grafting,

00:21:09.668 --> 00:21:13.071
by creating a copolymer,
where one type wants

00:21:13.071 --> 00:21:14.439
a form ionic bonds.

00:21:14.439 --> 00:21:15.507
I've cross-linked.

00:21:15.507 --> 00:21:18.810
I've made a cross-link built
into the polymer itself.

00:21:18.810 --> 00:21:23.315
The cross-link is going to be
linking the strands together,

00:21:23.315 --> 00:21:25.717
maybe within itself,
maybe with other strands

00:21:25.717 --> 00:21:28.754
but with an ionic bond.

00:21:28.754 --> 00:21:29.454
Right?

00:21:29.454 --> 00:21:33.191
But I've done it all just
built into the polymer.

00:21:33.191 --> 00:21:34.860
And that's why you can see here.

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:39.031
Look at this it's an
ethylene copolymer.

00:21:39.031 --> 00:21:39.531
All right?

00:21:39.531 --> 00:21:41.099
So ethylene is the backbone.

00:21:41.099 --> 00:21:44.002
The co is what's
coming out of the side,

00:21:44.002 --> 00:21:47.806
and that's an ionic bond, so
it's also called an ionomer.

00:21:47.806 --> 00:21:48.674
What's an ionomer?

00:21:48.674 --> 00:21:52.077
A polymer that has ionic
cross-links, ionic bonding

00:21:52.077 --> 00:21:52.611
cross-links.

00:21:52.611 --> 00:21:53.712
And what's cool about that?

00:21:53.712 --> 00:22:00.385
Well, you get the whole world
of ionic bonding now in the mix.

00:22:00.385 --> 00:22:02.287
And so what does that mean?

00:22:02.287 --> 00:22:05.691
Well, you could have it
be very strong, golf ball.

00:22:05.691 --> 00:22:08.160
You can have it be
thermally responsive.

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:08.660
Right?

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:12.464
You can tune at what
temperature these bonds break.

00:22:12.464 --> 00:22:13.865
There's all sorts
of flexibility,

00:22:13.865 --> 00:22:16.635
and you can see it right here
in how they're marketing it.

00:22:16.635 --> 00:22:19.404
It can do almost anything.

00:22:19.404 --> 00:22:23.675
Another example
of the copolymer--

00:22:23.675 --> 00:22:25.977
I'll give you a couple
examples of copolymers.

00:22:25.977 --> 00:22:32.651
This is one that you may
have seen, maybe many of you

00:22:32.651 --> 00:22:34.252
experienced, you
just don't remember.

00:22:34.252 --> 00:22:36.054
This is the diaper.

00:22:36.054 --> 00:22:36.588
Right?

00:22:36.588 --> 00:22:41.593
And so what what a
diaper is is it's

00:22:41.593 --> 00:22:46.164
got this copolymer made
out of acrylic acid.

00:22:46.164 --> 00:22:48.900
These are the monomers.

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:50.369
Notice the double bonds?

00:22:50.369 --> 00:22:53.905
Oh, we're going to take
advantage of those,

00:22:53.905 --> 00:22:55.640
and we're going
to make a polymer,

00:22:55.640 --> 00:22:58.377
but we're going to do it
in a controllable way.

00:22:58.377 --> 00:22:59.978
So we're going to
take these two mers,

00:22:59.978 --> 00:23:02.814
and we're going to control
how they come together.

00:23:02.814 --> 00:23:06.651
And very importantly in
these kinds of materials--

00:23:06.651 --> 00:23:09.988
they're broadly
called hydrogels.

00:23:09.988 --> 00:23:13.058
These are materials that
can absorb hundreds of times

00:23:13.058 --> 00:23:17.262
by weight, hundreds of
times water into them.

00:23:17.262 --> 00:23:18.063
Why?

00:23:18.063 --> 00:23:24.302
Because in one of the copolymers
you've got this sodium atom,

00:23:24.302 --> 00:23:26.371
and what happens is
there's the dry state.

00:23:26.371 --> 00:23:29.975
It's all curled up and the
chains want to be all together,

00:23:29.975 --> 00:23:31.443
but as soon as you
introduce water,

00:23:31.443 --> 00:23:35.647
those sodiums like
going into the water.

00:23:35.647 --> 00:23:36.348
And what happens?

00:23:36.348 --> 00:23:41.720
They leave behind this oxygen
that really wants the water

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:43.922
because it's a negative charge.

00:23:43.922 --> 00:23:46.158
And so you get a sodium
ion going in the solution.

00:23:46.158 --> 00:23:49.594
You get the solution
coming to the anion .

00:23:49.594 --> 00:23:54.499
And even more than that,
now if the sodium leaves,

00:23:54.499 --> 00:23:55.801
then what happens?

00:23:55.801 --> 00:23:58.837
Well, what happens is all
those negative charges are left

00:23:58.837 --> 00:24:01.173
and they repel each other.

00:24:01.173 --> 00:24:04.109
That's going to help this
whole thing want to expand.

00:24:04.109 --> 00:24:05.243
Right?

00:24:05.243 --> 00:24:12.918
So that's a diaper, 2% of
all landfill by the way.

00:24:12.918 --> 00:24:17.255
Now, so that's another
example of a copolymer

00:24:17.255 --> 00:24:20.091
and a really interesting way
of tuning the properties.

00:24:20.091 --> 00:24:23.662
In here it has to do
with, what did we do?

00:24:23.662 --> 00:24:27.833
We tuned the ionic
character of the backbone.

00:24:27.833 --> 00:24:30.168
We tuned the charge.

00:24:30.168 --> 00:24:32.904
We made it responsive it
in terms of its charge

00:24:32.904 --> 00:24:35.841
to something, in this case
water, the presence of water.

00:24:35.841 --> 00:24:37.042
That's cool.

00:24:37.042 --> 00:24:38.176
OK.

00:24:38.176 --> 00:24:42.214
Last one, and here I want to
talk about mechanical strength.

00:24:42.214 --> 00:24:47.419
So this is the tensile strength.

00:24:47.419 --> 00:24:50.555
So how far could
you pull this thing,

00:24:50.555 --> 00:24:53.391
this material before it breaks?

00:24:53.391 --> 00:24:56.495
And then here's the
elongation until it breaks,

00:24:56.495 --> 00:24:57.496
so it gives you a sense.

00:24:57.496 --> 00:24:59.631
Now the tensile strength,
this is how far can you

00:24:59.631 --> 00:25:01.566
pull it in the elastic regime?

00:25:01.566 --> 00:25:05.070
You can see that for
steel it's really high,

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:07.339
but you can't pull it
very far before it breaks.

00:25:07.339 --> 00:25:08.206
We know that, right?

00:25:08.206 --> 00:25:12.344
So steel is really strong,
but it's not very flexible.

00:25:12.344 --> 00:25:13.278
There is nylon.

00:25:13.278 --> 00:25:16.381
We did nylon, so it's kind
of not nearly as strong,

00:25:16.381 --> 00:25:19.217
but it has a lot of elongation.

00:25:19.217 --> 00:25:20.051
There's fiberglass.

00:25:20.051 --> 00:25:23.688
This is plastic
reinforced glass,

00:25:23.688 --> 00:25:26.157
where you can get
a lot of strength,

00:25:26.157 --> 00:25:28.627
very little flexibility, more
than steel, a little bit more

00:25:28.627 --> 00:25:29.361
than steel.

00:25:29.361 --> 00:25:30.729
Well actually, it
was a lot more,

00:25:30.729 --> 00:25:33.832
but still it's only
3% or 4% elongation,

00:25:33.832 --> 00:25:35.333
and here's cellophane.

00:25:35.333 --> 00:25:40.605
That can be a naturally
occurring polymer.

00:25:40.605 --> 00:25:42.908
You can also make it
synthetically in the mix.

00:25:42.908 --> 00:25:44.676
And I want to point out nitrile.

00:25:44.676 --> 00:25:48.446
Nitrile rubber is a really
interesting copolymer,

00:25:48.446 --> 00:25:51.283
and many of you may have seen
or used-- if you're in a lab,

00:25:51.283 --> 00:25:53.351
you might use nitrile
rubber gloves.

00:25:53.351 --> 00:25:56.688
You might have them
at home at the sink.

00:25:56.688 --> 00:25:58.557
And one of the advantages
of nitrile rubber

00:25:58.557 --> 00:26:03.161
is, again, this enormous
tunability by just picking

00:26:03.161 --> 00:26:04.563
how you copolymerized.

00:26:04.563 --> 00:26:07.699
How much of one did
you put in the other?

00:26:07.699 --> 00:26:12.804
And this is-- maybe
I'll use this board here

00:26:12.804 --> 00:26:15.941
because it's a really cool
copolymer, where you're

00:26:15.941 --> 00:26:19.544
putting in acrylonitrile.

00:26:19.544 --> 00:26:20.912
So here's what this looks like.

00:26:20.912 --> 00:26:28.987
So you put your CH2, your
CH, your double bond your CH,

00:26:28.987 --> 00:26:32.390
your CH2, and there it is.

00:26:32.390 --> 00:26:34.759
And that's going to repeat
some number of units

00:26:34.759 --> 00:26:40.432
that you control because it's
a block copolymer before you

00:26:40.432 --> 00:26:43.602
put this one in,
and here's the kick.

00:26:43.602 --> 00:26:47.539
It's got a triple
bond nitrogen on it.

00:26:47.539 --> 00:26:50.742
And that's going
to go a certain m.

00:26:50.742 --> 00:26:51.242
All right?

00:26:51.242 --> 00:26:54.546
Now, that comes from
acrylonitrile and butadiene,

00:26:54.546 --> 00:26:58.049
but again, it's the synthesis
to make the copolymer.

00:26:58.049 --> 00:27:00.619
It's the sequence.

00:27:00.619 --> 00:27:01.853
I keep on forgetting where.

00:27:01.853 --> 00:27:02.687
It's up there.

00:27:02.687 --> 00:27:06.524
It's the composition,
the architecture.

00:27:06.524 --> 00:27:08.026
How did I put those copoly--

00:27:08.026 --> 00:27:09.728
if you do this in
the right way, it's

00:27:09.728 --> 00:27:12.063
the amount of the
acrylonitrile that you put

00:27:12.063 --> 00:27:15.467
in will control the strength.

00:27:15.467 --> 00:27:18.203
It'll make it
stronger and stronger,

00:27:18.203 --> 00:27:21.072
because you've got this really
nice, strong bond in here,

00:27:21.072 --> 00:27:23.141
and this will give the
material a lot of strength.

00:27:23.141 --> 00:27:26.277
So that's why you can go up to,
you know, fairly reasonable.

00:27:26.277 --> 00:27:29.748
But then, how much
of this I put in

00:27:29.748 --> 00:27:31.883
is going to determine how
far you can stretch it,

00:27:31.883 --> 00:27:33.652
and so you balance those.

00:27:33.652 --> 00:27:38.023
And you can make a whole bunch
of kinds of nitrile rubber.

00:27:38.023 --> 00:27:40.925
OK, so I hope this has
given you some examples

00:27:40.925 --> 00:27:44.696
of the tunability of polymers.

00:27:44.696 --> 00:27:47.499
And you know, if you
take the bigger picture--

00:27:47.499 --> 00:27:48.900
and this is a
little hard to read.

00:27:48.900 --> 00:27:49.801
You know, it will
be in the slides,

00:27:49.801 --> 00:27:51.536
so you can look up
this paper here.

00:27:51.536 --> 00:27:53.838
It was published in
Science a few years ago--

00:27:53.838 --> 00:27:55.707
and you look at the
fracture toughness

00:27:55.707 --> 00:27:57.876
with the yield strength.

00:27:57.876 --> 00:27:58.543
So what is that?

00:27:58.543 --> 00:27:59.577
Well yield strength is--

00:27:59.577 --> 00:28:01.212
Remember, we know
that because it's

00:28:01.212 --> 00:28:04.115
when you're in the elastic
region, and you're pulling,

00:28:04.115 --> 00:28:06.618
and then you yield to
plastic deformation.

00:28:06.618 --> 00:28:07.318
Right?

00:28:07.318 --> 00:28:08.553
That's the yield strength.

00:28:08.553 --> 00:28:10.689
Now what about
fracture toughness?

00:28:10.689 --> 00:28:12.757
Well, that's if
you start a crack,

00:28:12.757 --> 00:28:15.460
does the thing tend
to crack more or not?

00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:17.829
That's the fracture
toughness basically, right?

00:28:17.829 --> 00:28:19.864
And so you could put
materials down on this plot.

00:28:19.864 --> 00:28:20.699
You've got ceramics.

00:28:20.699 --> 00:28:22.000
You've got concrete down here.

00:28:22.000 --> 00:28:24.769
You've got-- so you're
not going to be.

00:28:24.769 --> 00:28:28.540
So the polymers sit here.

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:29.040
All right?

00:28:29.040 --> 00:28:31.443
Here's metals, and
alloys, metallic gases.

00:28:31.443 --> 00:28:32.977
You can look at
this on your own,

00:28:32.977 --> 00:28:35.480
and look at each one of those,
but I just want to point out,

00:28:35.480 --> 00:28:37.849
polymers have a
fairly wide range,

00:28:37.849 --> 00:28:41.953
but there's so much
interest in going beyond.

00:28:41.953 --> 00:28:44.556
There's a lot of interest
in using polymers

00:28:44.556 --> 00:28:45.990
in many other applications.

00:28:45.990 --> 00:28:48.927
We can't get there yet
because we can't push it

00:28:48.927 --> 00:28:52.197
out here or maybe out here.

00:28:52.197 --> 00:28:56.167
We still need to figure
out how to tune it more.

00:28:56.167 --> 00:28:59.104
We've got all these ways
to control the properties,

00:28:59.104 --> 00:29:02.340
and we're still only at the
very beginning of understanding

00:29:02.340 --> 00:29:03.641
how to engineer polymers.

00:29:06.411 --> 00:29:09.848
Now, there is no better way
to make that point clear

00:29:09.848 --> 00:29:11.549
than to look at nature.

00:29:11.549 --> 00:29:12.317
All right?

00:29:12.317 --> 00:29:14.319
And I already
showed you the tree

00:29:14.319 --> 00:29:18.623
and you know, the examples of
nature as a polymer engineer.

00:29:18.623 --> 00:29:23.328
I want to talk about that a
little more because nature

00:29:23.328 --> 00:29:27.132
is not just a polymer engineer.

00:29:27.132 --> 00:29:29.234
Nature-- I'm going to
write this down nature.

00:29:29.234 --> 00:29:34.472
Nature is a polymer
engineer gone wild,

00:29:34.472 --> 00:29:35.540
and I'll show you why.

00:29:35.540 --> 00:29:36.307
Polymer engineer.

00:29:45.416 --> 00:29:49.154
Humans, what can humans do?

00:29:49.154 --> 00:29:53.458
Well, we are a natural polymer
engineer material, but what can

00:29:53.458 --> 00:29:56.528
we make with all of what
I've just shown you?

00:29:56.528 --> 00:29:58.596
What can we make?

00:29:58.596 --> 00:30:02.500
I can put like
one, or maybe two,

00:30:02.500 --> 00:30:04.536
or if you really go
into the research,

00:30:04.536 --> 00:30:08.640
you get three mers,
trying to control

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:10.441
where they are in the chain.

00:30:10.441 --> 00:30:10.942
All right?

00:30:10.942 --> 00:30:12.243
You've got three, maybe two.

00:30:12.243 --> 00:30:14.512
We have really two
in most materials.

00:30:14.512 --> 00:30:17.081
Copolymer-- we're so
proud of this nitrile.

00:30:17.081 --> 00:30:22.754
Two mers, and we control them,
and we make rubber sheets.

00:30:22.754 --> 00:30:26.024
But nature-- but then
they're everywhere.

00:30:26.024 --> 00:30:28.827
They're everywhere is just that.

00:30:28.827 --> 00:30:29.727
All right?

00:30:29.727 --> 00:30:31.296
So nature can have--

00:30:31.296 --> 00:30:47.111
so humans have the same
functional group every stop.

00:30:47.111 --> 00:30:47.912
All right?

00:30:47.912 --> 00:30:49.147
OK, that's one mer.

00:30:49.147 --> 00:30:53.551
Maybe I've got two,
maybe two or three.

00:30:53.551 --> 00:31:06.731
Nature can have different
groups, and here's the key.

00:31:06.731 --> 00:31:08.566
It can have them everywhere.

00:31:11.369 --> 00:31:13.171
OK?

00:31:13.171 --> 00:31:22.647
So what I have is I have
many, many more possibilities.

00:31:22.647 --> 00:31:24.315
I mean, we have these
possibilities too,

00:31:24.315 --> 00:31:27.018
we just can't control it.

00:31:27.018 --> 00:31:27.652
All right?

00:31:27.652 --> 00:31:30.154
So if I look at--
like let's go back

00:31:30.154 --> 00:31:32.223
to condensation
polymerization because this

00:31:32.223 --> 00:31:34.659
is what nature does.

00:31:34.659 --> 00:31:38.129
So this was what I
drew for you on Monday.

00:31:38.129 --> 00:31:40.198
There's a dicarboxylic acid.

00:31:40.198 --> 00:31:42.233
Here we're making nylon.

00:31:42.233 --> 00:31:44.002
And a diamine, and
we're making polyamide.

00:31:44.002 --> 00:31:45.637
And remember, the box--

00:31:45.637 --> 00:31:47.972
in nylon 66, the box
is 6 carbon atoms.

00:31:47.972 --> 00:31:50.875
I call that boring.

00:31:50.875 --> 00:31:53.044
It's at six carbon
atoms from hydrogen,

00:31:53.044 --> 00:31:54.312
but that's kind of boring.

00:31:54.312 --> 00:31:59.050
But in nature, this
box is an amino acid.

00:31:59.050 --> 00:32:01.452
That's much more interesting.

00:32:01.452 --> 00:32:03.788
That's much more
interesting because if you

00:32:03.788 --> 00:32:08.126
look at an amino acid, and
this is an amino acid--

00:32:08.126 --> 00:32:09.127
why is it an amino acid?

00:32:09.127 --> 00:32:12.130
Well, it's got an amine group
here and a carboxylic acid

00:32:12.130 --> 00:32:15.333
there, so it's an amino acid.

00:32:15.333 --> 00:32:18.536
But see, here's the thing, R.

00:32:18.536 --> 00:32:22.941
This is nature's "the box."

00:32:22.941 --> 00:32:24.208
We can put six carbons in.

00:32:24.208 --> 00:32:25.543
We're really proud of ourselves.

00:32:25.543 --> 00:32:32.050
Nature can put almost anything
it wants for R. All right?

00:32:32.050 --> 00:32:43.261
So R, just to spell this
out, is nature's choice,

00:32:43.261 --> 00:32:44.662
and I'll show you
what it chooses.

00:32:44.662 --> 00:32:52.737
Nature's choice-- because there
are hundreds of amino acids,

00:32:52.737 --> 00:33:01.245
and this R group can do many
things, but just 20 is all we

00:33:01.245 --> 00:33:02.914
need to make proteins.

00:33:02.914 --> 00:33:06.417
Most proteins are made out
of just essentially 20.

00:33:06.417 --> 00:33:08.219
But if you do the math--

00:33:08.219 --> 00:33:10.855
I think I have the math here--

00:33:10.855 --> 00:33:13.291
and you take-- let's see.

00:33:13.291 --> 00:33:17.862
OK, if I take two amino acids.

00:33:17.862 --> 00:33:19.330
Let's say I take
two amino acids,

00:33:19.330 --> 00:33:21.065
so I've got two different R's.

00:33:21.065 --> 00:33:30.475
Two amino acids, and
I've got a length is 2,

00:33:30.475 --> 00:33:32.610
and this is called
the dipeptide.

00:33:32.610 --> 00:33:34.145
I'll tell you why in a sec.

00:33:34.145 --> 00:33:36.581
OK, but let's compare this now.

00:33:36.581 --> 00:33:41.586
Now I've got 20 amino acids, and
I just told you that 20 is what

00:33:41.586 --> 00:33:46.057
nature makes most proteins
out of, 20 different R's.

00:33:46.057 --> 00:33:47.225
There's an amino acid.

00:33:47.225 --> 00:33:49.494
R is nature's choice.

00:33:49.494 --> 00:33:56.901
But see, if I've got 20,
then I've got 20 squared

00:33:56.901 --> 00:33:59.904
equals 400 dipeptides.

00:33:59.904 --> 00:34:02.540
I've made a two-unit polymer.

00:34:02.540 --> 00:34:03.341
It's not a polymer.

00:34:03.341 --> 00:34:06.077
It's a peptide.

00:34:06.077 --> 00:34:09.347
But now, what if I take
the 20, and I've got--

00:34:09.347 --> 00:34:18.456
but now I've got, let's
say, 1,000 units long,

00:34:18.456 --> 00:34:25.396
then I would have 20 to the
1,000 possibilities, which is

00:34:25.396 --> 00:34:32.170
10 to the 1,300 combinations.

00:34:32.170 --> 00:34:39.043
So 10 to the 1,300
possibilities--

00:34:39.043 --> 00:34:41.579
that's because
there's 1,000 units,

00:34:41.579 --> 00:34:43.547
and I've got 20
amino acids, right?

00:34:43.547 --> 00:34:46.684
But now you think,
how do you possibly

00:34:46.684 --> 00:34:48.853
think about what to do?

00:34:48.853 --> 00:34:51.155
That is nature.

00:34:51.155 --> 00:34:54.324
It's had 1 billion
years, and it's

00:34:54.324 --> 00:34:57.361
given us the world that
we live in, that we know.

00:34:57.361 --> 00:35:01.866
It's messed with
these combinations

00:35:01.866 --> 00:35:04.435
in a way that's impossible
for us to even understand.

00:35:04.435 --> 00:35:07.905
That's why I say, nature is
a polymer engineer gone wild.

00:35:07.905 --> 00:35:13.544
It's got almost limitless
flexibility, and it's used it.

00:35:13.544 --> 00:35:14.512
Right?

00:35:14.512 --> 00:35:16.781
Now, how does nature
make its polymer?

00:35:16.781 --> 00:35:18.516
Well, its condensation
polymerization.

00:35:18.516 --> 00:35:21.953
This is just what we saw.

00:35:21.953 --> 00:35:23.621
Look at that.

00:35:23.621 --> 00:35:25.156
This is what an amino acid is.

00:35:25.156 --> 00:35:27.325
It's got this
carboxylic, so there's

00:35:27.325 --> 00:35:30.228
the OH group that can
condense with the H

00:35:30.228 --> 00:35:32.430
here and form the link.

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:33.264
There it is.

00:35:33.264 --> 00:35:35.199
That's called a peptide bond.

00:35:35.199 --> 00:35:37.435
When two amino acids come
together, that's a CN.

00:35:37.435 --> 00:35:39.003
That's a peptide bond.

00:35:39.003 --> 00:35:40.271
Right?

00:35:40.271 --> 00:35:41.806
But look, this had R1.

00:35:41.806 --> 00:35:49.814
That had R2, and I've got
20 different amino acids

00:35:49.814 --> 00:35:50.748
to choose from.

00:35:50.748 --> 00:35:53.751
The possibilities are endless.

00:35:53.751 --> 00:36:01.759
So the protein synthesis
is condensation pol--

00:36:01.759 --> 00:36:02.426
you knew that.

00:36:02.426 --> 00:36:04.562
You knew it couldn't be
anything else because there

00:36:04.562 --> 00:36:06.063
is no double bond.

00:36:06.063 --> 00:36:09.467
Where would I have done
a radical initiation

00:36:09.467 --> 00:36:11.536
with these amino acids?

00:36:11.536 --> 00:36:14.505
If I'm nature, I've got to
make it with condensation

00:36:14.505 --> 00:36:15.706
polymerization.

00:36:15.706 --> 00:36:18.476
That's how we are made.

00:36:18.476 --> 00:36:22.180
OK, now, so just to give
you a sense, so the R--

00:36:22.180 --> 00:36:24.015
I'm not going to go
through this, obviously.

00:36:24.015 --> 00:36:25.183
I'm just giving this
to you as if you

00:36:25.183 --> 00:36:26.284
want to read more about it.

00:36:26.284 --> 00:36:29.687
There's some wonderful
charts here, 20 amino acids,

00:36:29.687 --> 00:36:33.191
the 20 that are most common
and that nature puts together.

00:36:33.191 --> 00:36:34.859
And what they've done
that's really nice

00:36:34.859 --> 00:36:36.360
here is they've
grouped them, right?

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:39.897
Because what did
nature choose to do?

00:36:39.897 --> 00:36:43.367
How is it most utilizing the
properties, the tunability

00:36:43.367 --> 00:36:44.268
in these amino acids.

00:36:44.268 --> 00:36:45.903
I said there were 20,
but most proteins are--

00:36:45.903 --> 00:36:47.271
I'd say there are
hundreds, but most proteins

00:36:47.271 --> 00:36:48.673
are made from these 20.

00:36:48.673 --> 00:36:54.679
It gives you the tunability in
having maybe non-polar groups.

00:36:54.679 --> 00:36:55.313
Right?

00:36:55.313 --> 00:36:57.582
So it might be then hydrophobic.

00:36:57.582 --> 00:37:03.754
Polar groups, hydrophilic--
maybe you can put,

00:37:03.754 --> 00:37:08.159
nature can put, R groups in
there that maybe have a charge,

00:37:08.159 --> 00:37:10.695
or that lose or take an ion.

00:37:10.695 --> 00:37:13.631
All right, so it can
play with the charge,

00:37:13.631 --> 00:37:15.533
and it has done all of that.

00:37:15.533 --> 00:37:17.935
It can have groups that become
acids, that make something

00:37:17.935 --> 00:37:20.204
acidic, and so forth.

00:37:20.204 --> 00:37:22.707
OK, so that's cool.

00:37:22.707 --> 00:37:24.942
What has nature done?

00:37:24.942 --> 00:37:28.913
Well, I got to show you
this spider because--

00:37:28.913 --> 00:37:30.748
and if you're interested,
Professor Bueller

00:37:30.748 --> 00:37:33.184
does some wonderful
work on spider silk--

00:37:33.184 --> 00:37:37.655
because I think this is a
great example of something

00:37:37.655 --> 00:37:43.794
that nature can do that gives us
a sense of how far away we are.

00:37:43.794 --> 00:37:49.934
Like I said, we're very proud
of this, and we want to do more.

00:37:49.934 --> 00:37:52.837
And so we take our two mers,
and we mix them together,

00:37:52.837 --> 00:37:54.372
and we make branches,
and maybe we're

00:37:54.372 --> 00:37:56.607
going to try to add a third.

00:37:56.607 --> 00:38:00.144
Meanwhile, nature has had a lot
more flexibility and a lot more

00:38:00.144 --> 00:38:00.845
time.

00:38:00.845 --> 00:38:01.579
What can it do?

00:38:01.579 --> 00:38:03.581
Well, here's spider silk.

00:38:03.581 --> 00:38:05.349
Now, this is a spider.

00:38:05.349 --> 00:38:10.254
Spider is an incredible
polymer synthesis machine.

00:38:10.254 --> 00:38:12.623
It's an incredible
polymer synthesis machine.

00:38:12.623 --> 00:38:14.725
And here it is weaving
a web, and I love this--

00:38:14.725 --> 00:38:15.092
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:38:15.092 --> 00:38:16.460
--because I think it's so cool.

00:38:16.460 --> 00:38:18.562
OK, there's music, I guess.

00:38:18.562 --> 00:38:20.765
I forgot about that,
and there it is.

00:38:20.765 --> 00:38:21.599
Now watch.

00:38:21.599 --> 00:38:25.202
Out of here, this is
the back of the spider.

00:38:25.202 --> 00:38:26.971
There it is right there.

00:38:26.971 --> 00:38:28.239
It's making protein.

00:38:28.239 --> 00:38:31.042
That's called spider silk,
but these are proteins.

00:38:31.042 --> 00:38:32.209
These are polymers.

00:38:32.209 --> 00:38:35.513
It is doing condensation
polymerization right there,

00:38:35.513 --> 00:38:38.015
and then there's all sorts of
structural stuff that it does.

00:38:38.015 --> 00:38:38.482
Right?

00:38:38.482 --> 00:38:39.684
It's got a specialized hook.

00:38:39.684 --> 00:38:40.584
It knows how to step.

00:38:40.584 --> 00:38:41.686
It creates-- look at that.

00:38:41.686 --> 00:38:43.721
There's a branch place
where it knows to put it.

00:38:43.721 --> 00:38:44.221
Right?

00:38:44.221 --> 00:38:47.558
It's already created the glue.

00:38:47.558 --> 00:38:50.995
So not only is it putting
this spider silk out there,

00:38:50.995 --> 00:38:54.031
but it can put other
types of polymer

00:38:54.031 --> 00:38:55.199
depending on what it needs.

00:38:55.199 --> 00:38:58.002
Does it need something
really sticky, less sticky?

00:38:58.002 --> 00:38:58.536
Right?

00:38:58.536 --> 00:39:00.037
And so there it is
weaving its web,

00:39:00.037 --> 00:39:02.873
and it's generating
this polymer on the fly.

00:39:02.873 --> 00:39:04.975
It's doing condensation
polymerization.

00:39:04.975 --> 00:39:07.311
Now a couple properties
about spider silk.

00:39:07.311 --> 00:39:10.348
OK, so here we go.

00:39:10.348 --> 00:39:10.981
Let's see.

00:39:10.981 --> 00:39:19.590
So spider silk, this is just one
example of what nature can do.

00:39:19.590 --> 00:39:21.592
It's five times
stronger than steel.

00:39:27.198 --> 00:39:29.567
Remember the mechanical
strength chart,

00:39:29.567 --> 00:39:31.502
nothing was stronger than steel.

00:39:31.502 --> 00:39:34.405
Spider silk is five
times stronger.

00:39:34.405 --> 00:39:39.009
Just to give you sense, the
example, I found that I like.

00:39:39.009 --> 00:39:42.012
If you had a spider silk
that was a pencil width,

00:39:42.012 --> 00:39:45.383
and you made a strand, it would
stop a Boeing 747 in midair.

00:39:45.383 --> 00:39:47.585
That's how strong it is.

00:39:47.585 --> 00:39:50.254
Oh, it keeps strength--

00:39:50.254 --> 00:40:01.899
here's another thing-- below
40 degrees C. We can't do that.

00:40:01.899 --> 00:40:04.935
Just take a rubber ball and put
it at that low of a temperature

00:40:04.935 --> 00:40:05.803
and try to bounce it.

00:40:05.803 --> 00:40:07.171
It's going to shatter.

00:40:07.171 --> 00:40:07.705
Right?

00:40:07.705 --> 00:40:11.409
Spider silk can keep that
strength and not break.

00:40:11.409 --> 00:40:14.945
Its elastic, so it's got--

00:40:14.945 --> 00:40:19.717
throughout all of this, it's
got an elastic property of 4x,

00:40:19.717 --> 00:40:22.186
so it can be stretched to four
times its original strength.

00:40:26.157 --> 00:40:27.725
Compare that with nitrile.

00:40:27.725 --> 00:40:31.162
Nitrile rubber could
also go to very, very

00:40:31.162 --> 00:40:34.432
high elastic elongation.

00:40:34.432 --> 00:40:34.932
All right?

00:40:34.932 --> 00:40:37.601
So if you go back to nitrile--

00:40:37.601 --> 00:40:39.437
here it is in the table.

00:40:39.437 --> 00:40:41.338
We're very happy with
this, but this only

00:40:41.338 --> 00:40:44.008
had two monomers to play with.

00:40:44.008 --> 00:40:44.542
Right?

00:40:44.542 --> 00:40:46.544
We played with two monomers,
and we got to here,

00:40:46.544 --> 00:40:49.580
and look at the sacrifice
in the strength.

00:40:49.580 --> 00:40:52.082
Look at how much we had
to sacrifice strength.

00:40:52.082 --> 00:40:53.918
Spiders don't have to do that.

00:40:53.918 --> 00:40:57.488
OK, and oh, here's the last one.

00:40:57.488 --> 00:41:05.362
Fully recycles-- now the
thing is that this is actually

00:41:05.362 --> 00:41:06.564
kind of incredible.

00:41:06.564 --> 00:41:09.800
Spider webs get dusty.

00:41:09.800 --> 00:41:12.336
They lose their stickiness.

00:41:12.336 --> 00:41:16.307
So many spiders know
this and just simply

00:41:16.307 --> 00:41:18.976
have to weave a
new web every day,

00:41:18.976 --> 00:41:21.378
but they don't leave
the old web there.

00:41:21.378 --> 00:41:26.550
They actually eat it, and
they fully recycle it, fully.

00:41:26.550 --> 00:41:27.051
Right?

00:41:27.051 --> 00:41:30.154
They eat the web, fully
recycle it, process it,

00:41:30.154 --> 00:41:34.058
have this condensation
polymerization work,

00:41:34.058 --> 00:41:36.093
and make a new one the next day.

00:41:36.093 --> 00:41:38.362
All right?

00:41:38.362 --> 00:41:41.866
So we don't come
close to this spider.

00:41:41.866 --> 00:41:46.570
We don't even come anywhere near
it in terms of where we are.

00:41:46.570 --> 00:41:48.906
Even though I gave you
all these wonderful things

00:41:48.906 --> 00:41:50.407
that we're doing
with engineering,

00:41:50.407 --> 00:41:53.777
we still have so far that
we could go if we could just

00:41:53.777 --> 00:41:56.313
figure out how nature works.

00:41:56.313 --> 00:41:57.915
OK?

00:41:57.915 --> 00:42:00.751
And this gets me to
what we do, and so I've

00:42:00.751 --> 00:42:02.720
got my "why this matters" now.

00:42:02.720 --> 00:42:07.458
And so a spider eats its
web, fully recycles it,

00:42:07.458 --> 00:42:09.193
spins a new web the next day.

00:42:09.193 --> 00:42:14.598
Here's what we do
with our polymers.

00:42:14.598 --> 00:42:17.635
I already talked
about the oceans.

00:42:17.635 --> 00:42:20.271
Here's what we do on land.

00:42:20.271 --> 00:42:21.572
These are tires.

00:42:21.572 --> 00:42:23.374
Now, tires are very
difficult to recycle.

00:42:23.374 --> 00:42:23.874
Why?

00:42:23.874 --> 00:42:26.243
Because they're too vulcanized.

00:42:26.243 --> 00:42:27.578
They've got too much cross-link.

00:42:27.578 --> 00:42:30.381
Remember, if the cross-link
density is too strong,

00:42:30.381 --> 00:42:35.920
which you need to make a tire,
then you can't recycle it.

00:42:35.920 --> 00:42:37.755
And in fact, what happens is--

00:42:37.755 --> 00:42:39.490
and there's a tire mound.

00:42:39.490 --> 00:42:41.258
Here's it is from a
satellite picture.

00:42:41.258 --> 00:42:43.093
Those are tires.

00:42:43.093 --> 00:42:44.128
Those are tire mounds.

00:42:44.128 --> 00:42:45.896
Here's what happens
when one catches fire.

00:42:45.896 --> 00:42:47.998
Here's what happens
when many catch fire.

00:42:47.998 --> 00:42:48.966
All right?

00:42:48.966 --> 00:42:53.504
It's actually a very hard fire
to control once they catch.

00:42:53.504 --> 00:42:55.773
But we don't really
know how to recycle them

00:42:55.773 --> 00:42:57.441
well yet.

00:42:57.449 --> 00:43:00.411
What can we do?

00:43:00.411 --> 00:43:03.581
On the science and engineering
side, the fact of the matter

00:43:03.581 --> 00:43:05.349
is there is a lot of
work to do, but there

00:43:05.349 --> 00:43:06.717
are promising directions.

00:43:06.717 --> 00:43:09.253
So I wanted to leave you
with a little bit of that,

00:43:09.253 --> 00:43:10.988
and I'm not going to
go into great detail.

00:43:10.988 --> 00:43:13.157
I just want to show you,
and there's references here

00:43:13.157 --> 00:43:13.724
you can look.

00:43:13.724 --> 00:43:16.160
This was published a couple
of years ago in Nature.

00:43:16.160 --> 00:43:21.165
One direction is in
self-healing polymers.

00:43:21.165 --> 00:43:22.733
This is a very
exciting direction.

00:43:22.733 --> 00:43:23.534
What can we do?

00:43:23.534 --> 00:43:26.670
Well, we can go away from
this whole single-use idea

00:43:26.670 --> 00:43:28.772
and make stuff last longer.

00:43:28.772 --> 00:43:29.273
All right?

00:43:29.273 --> 00:43:31.308
So that would be beneficial.

00:43:31.308 --> 00:43:33.811
Maybe not if it ends
up in the ocean,

00:43:33.811 --> 00:43:38.015
but in terms of just how long
we can use these materials.

00:43:38.015 --> 00:43:40.784
So one direction is, well,
you've got these polymer

00:43:40.784 --> 00:43:43.253
networks, and you incorporate
little gels, little beads

00:43:43.253 --> 00:43:46.156
in here, but the
beads don't open up

00:43:46.156 --> 00:43:48.792
until a crack comes along.

00:43:48.792 --> 00:43:50.394
So they're sensitive to a crack.

00:43:50.394 --> 00:43:53.530
And when they feel a crack in
the material, they open up,

00:43:53.530 --> 00:43:57.334
and they pour a healing
liquid that then solidifies.

00:43:57.334 --> 00:43:58.969
That's a self-healing
kind of approach.

00:43:58.969 --> 00:44:00.537
You could do that
at different scales,

00:44:00.537 --> 00:44:02.139
all the way down to the strand.

00:44:02.139 --> 00:44:03.507
You can do that
on larger scales.

00:44:03.507 --> 00:44:06.110
And here's a whole system
where there's actually

00:44:06.110 --> 00:44:09.446
this healing material being
flowed through a polymer

00:44:09.446 --> 00:44:13.083
structure, just like
arteries in our body.

00:44:13.083 --> 00:44:16.186
Again, always there to
try to heal the material.

00:44:16.186 --> 00:44:17.621
Right?

00:44:17.621 --> 00:44:20.791
Another direction is
in fully recovering.

00:44:20.791 --> 00:44:22.960
And again, I don't want to
go into full detail here.

00:44:22.960 --> 00:44:24.561
You can look up
some of this stuff.

00:44:24.561 --> 00:44:26.930
This was published last year.

00:44:26.930 --> 00:44:30.000
Can we take the
polymer and chemically

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:33.504
decompose it all the
way back to the monomer?

00:44:33.504 --> 00:44:35.539
Can we go back to
where we started?

00:44:35.539 --> 00:44:38.375
Can we do what the spider does?

00:44:38.375 --> 00:44:38.876
Right?

00:44:38.876 --> 00:44:41.879
The answer is no, not today.

00:44:41.879 --> 00:44:45.249
But if we could,
that would open up

00:44:45.249 --> 00:44:48.318
a whole lot of
doors for recycling

00:44:48.318 --> 00:44:49.553
that are closed today.

00:44:49.553 --> 00:44:52.489
Can we do this in a
way that is efficient?

00:44:52.489 --> 00:44:53.691
Right?

00:44:53.691 --> 00:44:56.894
And then, another
direction of work

00:44:56.894 --> 00:44:58.529
that I think is
very important is

00:44:58.529 --> 00:45:02.499
in making thermosets so
heavily cross-linked so you

00:45:02.499 --> 00:45:04.668
get all the hardness and
all the properties you need

00:45:04.668 --> 00:45:07.538
of the polymer that's heavily
cross-linked, but easily

00:45:07.538 --> 00:45:09.206
breakable in the cross link.

00:45:09.206 --> 00:45:12.142
And there is good work
going on in this direction.

00:45:12.142 --> 00:45:15.312
Can we make
degradable thermosets?

00:45:15.312 --> 00:45:17.581
That's another really
important direction.

00:45:17.581 --> 00:45:20.084
And last, we should
be encouraging people,

00:45:20.084 --> 00:45:22.186
if you can't do any
of this, at least take

00:45:22.186 --> 00:45:24.922
the polymer out of the landfill
and make something with it.

00:45:24.922 --> 00:45:27.624
And there are projects-- you can
look at here, Waste Management

00:45:27.624 --> 00:45:30.527
Journal, in making
bricks out of polymers,

00:45:30.527 --> 00:45:32.930
incorporating them into
construction materials.

00:45:32.930 --> 00:45:33.430
Right?

00:45:33.430 --> 00:45:37.634
These are important directions,
and we need a lot more

00:45:37.634 --> 00:45:40.204
hopefully in the very near term.

00:45:40.204 --> 00:45:40.270
hopefully in the very near term.

00:45:40.270 --> 00:45:42.206
No more just talking
about alignment.

00:45:42.206 --> 00:45:43.774
OK, have a good weekend.

